    {
      "sample_index": 4,
      "text": "To find the ratio of the areas of triangles \\( \\triangle PQT \\) and \\( \\triangle PTR \\), we need to use the fact that the area of a triangle is proportional to the base when the height is the same. Here, the height from \\( P \\) to \\( QR \\) is the same for both triangles, so the ratio of the areas is the same as the ratio of the lengths of the bases \\( QT \\) and \\( TR \\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\( QT = 6 \\)\n- \\( TR = 10 \\)\n\nThe ratio of the areas of \\( \\triangle PQT \\) to \\( \\triangle PTR \\) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT}{\\text{Area of } \\triangle PTR} = \\frac{QT}{TR} = \\frac{6}{10} = \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the areas is \\( 3:5 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code to ensure the calculation is correct.\n```python\n# Given lengths\nQT = 6\nTR = 10\n\n# Ratio of the areas\nratio = QT / TR\n\n# Simplifying the ratio to its lowest terms\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Find the greatest common divisor\ng = gcd(QT, TR)\n\n# Simplify the ratio\nsimplified_ratio = (QT // g, TR // g)\nprint(simplified_ratio)\n```\n```output\n(3, 5)\n```\nThe ratio of the areas of \\( \\triangle PQT \\) to \\( \\triangle PTR \\) is \\(\\boxed{3:5}\\).",
