      "text": "To find the ratio of the areas of triangles \\( \\triangle PQT \\) and \\( \\triangle PTR \\), we first need to determine the base lengths and the heights from point \\( P \\) to the lines \\( QT \\) and \\( TR \\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\( QT = 6 \\)\n- \\( TR = 10 \\)\n\nLet's denote:\n- \\( QR = QT + TR = 6 + 10 = 16 \\)\n- \\( QT = 6 \\)\n- \\( TR = 10 \\)\n\nSince \\( T \\) is on \\( QR \\), we can use the fact that the areas of triangles are proportional to the lengths of their bases when the height from \\( P \\) is the same.\n\nThe area of \\( \\triangle PQT \\) can be written as:\n\\[ \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = \\frac{1}{2} \\times QT \\times \\text{height from } P \\text{ to } QT \\]\n\nThe area of \\( \\triangle PTR \\) can be written as:\n\\[ \\text{Area of } \\triangle PTR = \\frac{1}{2} \\times TR \\times \\text{height from } P \\text{ to } TR \\]\n\nSince the height from \\( P \\) to both \\( QT \\) and \\( TR \\) is the same, the ratio of the areas of \\( \\triangle PQT \\) to \\( \\triangle PTR \\) is simply the ratio of their bases:\n\\[ \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{QT}{TR} = \\frac{6}{10} = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nSo, the ratio of the area of \\( \\triangle PQT \\) to the area of \\( \\triangle PTR \\) is \\( 3:5 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given lengths\nQT = 6\nTR = 10\n\n# Calculate the ratio of the areas\nratio = QT / TR\nprint(ratio)\n```\n```output\n0.6\n```\nThe ratio of the area of \\( \\triangle PQT \\) to the area of \\( \\triangle PTR \\) is \\( \\frac{3}{5} \\), which is equivalent to 0.6 when expressed as a decimal. Thus, the final answer in the form \\( x:y \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3:5}\n\\]",
